1
00:00:00,758 --> 00:00:04,122
This is SANCHAR, for problem statement SIH26123

2
00:00:04,142 --> 00:00:06,006
from Bharat Electronics Limited:

3
00:00:06,026 --> 00:00:08,751
a fleet of autonomous mobile robots with no master.

4
00:00:08,771 --> 00:00:10,880
No server, no leader, no broker.

5
00:00:11,358 --> 00:00:15,757
Today, one central server plans the whole fleet over Wi-Fi.

6
00:00:15,777 --> 00:00:18,050
That means network latency, Wi-Fi dead zones

7
00:00:18,070 --> 00:00:22,053
and a single point of failure: when the link drops, every robot stops.

8
00:00:22,073 --> 00:00:24,105
The targets: zero inter-robot collisions,

9
00:00:24,125 --> 00:00:27,080
and at least 20% faster than stop-and-wait.

10
00:00:27,758 --> 00:00:29,997
The dashboard shows live positions and battery,

11
00:00:30,017 --> 00:00:32,349
and it only listens to the robots' radio.

12
00:00:32,369 --> 00:00:34,527
Each line is a robot's broadcast plan.

13
00:00:34,547 --> 00:00:38,635
At single-lane choke points, a corridor gate passes one direction at a time,

14
00:00:38,655 --> 00:00:41,921
and push, swap and yield rules with a wait-for cycle breaker

15
00:00:41,941 --> 00:00:44,013
resolve deadlocks in real time.

16
00:00:44,491 --> 00:00:46,734
A pallet blocks an aisle.

17
00:00:46,754 --> 00:00:49,457
The robot that sees it broadcasts an obstacle report,

18
00:00:49,477 --> 00:00:50,278
robots re-route,

19
00:00:50,298 --> 00:00:54,380
and blocked orders are released and re-auctioned on a CRDT task board.

20
00:00:54,858 --> 00:00:56,954
Now the Wi-Fi dies over part of the floor,

21
00:00:56,974 --> 00:00:59,817
and nearby robots keep talking over short-range links.

22
00:00:59,837 --> 00:01:02,931
Then a robot fails.

23
00:01:02,951 --> 00:01:05,084
Its order re-opens, another robot wins it,

24
00:01:05,104 --> 00:01:06,934
and the rest route around it.

25
00:01:06,954 --> 00:01:09,013
Collisions: still zero.

26
00:01:09,491 --> 00:01:11,281
Every robot runs the same edge node,

27
00:01:11,301 --> 00:01:14,048
designed for Raspberry Pi or Jetson class hardware.

28
00:01:14,068 --> 00:01:16,707
It shares its localization and intent with peers,

29
00:01:16,727 --> 00:01:19,046
bids for orders in a decentralized auction,

30
00:01:19,066 --> 00:01:21,730
and does multi-agent path planning on board:

31
00:01:21,750 --> 00:01:23,405
space-time A* for its route,

32
00:01:23,425 --> 00:01:27,547
and Guided-PIBT, priority inheritance with backtracking, for each step.

33
00:01:28,025 --> 00:01:30,512
Every round, each robot broadcasts a Hello:

34
00:01:30,532 --> 00:01:35,876
its position, the node it claims next, its priority, battery, and which peers it heard.

35
00:01:35,896 --> 00:01:37,727
An Intent carries its next ten path nodes.

36
00:01:37,747 --> 00:01:40,834
Every packet is signed with HMAC-SHA256

37
00:01:40,854 --> 00:01:43,330
and checked against an anti-replay window,

38
00:01:43,350 --> 00:01:48,147
and robots relay each other's messages up to two hops, across dead zones.

39
00:01:48,625 --> 00:01:51,086
Motion uses a discrete-time kinematic model:

40
00:01:51,106 --> 00:01:53,963
one metre per second on a one-metre roadmap.

41
00:01:53,983 --> 00:01:57,385
Each one-second tick has a claim round and a move round.

42
00:01:57,405 --> 00:01:59,891
A robot enters a node only if it claimed it,

43
00:01:59,911 --> 00:02:01,759
every peer acknowledged hearing it,

44
00:02:01,779 --> 00:02:04,929
and no peer's position, claim or reach set conflicts.

45
00:02:04,949 --> 00:02:08,680
So a lost packet can only cause a wait, never a collision.

46
00:02:09,158 --> 00:02:14,351
Across 11 scenarios, 30 seeds each, SANCHAR had zero collisions in all 330 runs.

47
00:02:14,371 --> 00:02:16,825
Measured with 95% confidence intervals,

48
00:02:16,845 --> 00:02:20,932
six robots on overlapping paths finish 25% faster than stop-and-wait,

49
00:02:20,952 --> 00:02:24,171
twelve robots 56%, and live orders 43%.

50
00:02:24,191 --> 00:02:27,355
At a single-lane intersection, stop-and-wait gridlocked every time.

51
00:02:27,375 --> 00:02:29,637
Our weak case: an isolated two-way chokepoint,

52
00:02:29,657 --> 00:02:32,447
where central zone control is 16% faster.

53
00:02:32,925 --> 00:02:36,927
We also run one process per robot over real UDP and kill one mid-run:

54
00:02:36,947 --> 00:02:39,190
the rest still deliver every order.

55
00:02:39,210 --> 00:02:41,983
Each decision takes under 2 milliseconds on a laptop,

56
00:02:42,003 --> 00:02:45,518
with 2 to 5 kilobytes of radio traffic per robot per second.

57
00:02:45,538 --> 00:02:48,985
Next, at the finale: continuous velocity and acceleration profiles,

58
00:02:49,005 --> 00:02:53,021
direction batching at choke points, and tests on Raspberry Pi hardware.

59
00:02:53,041 --> 00:02:57,680
SANCHAR: a fleet that keeps moving when the network dies.
